Subject:      Accept `\-` (escaped dash) in grammar character classes
Applies to:   TheTom/llama-cpp-turboquant @ fb2cc35ab3507a5aab56fe593620cd96d8f45384
              (src/llama-grammar.cpp, parse_char)

WHAT IT FIXES
=============
`parse_char` accepts `\\ " [ ]` (plus t/r/n/x/u/U) as escapes and THROWS
"unknown escape" on anything else — including `\-`, which JSON Schema
`pattern` values use routinely for a literal dash inside a character class
(e.g. `[A-Za-z0-9_\-.~:@+]`, an identifier pattern MCP tool schemas carry).
The schema-to-grammar converter passes the pattern through (logging
"Unsupported pattern syntax", which degrades nothing by itself), and then the
parser rejects the whole 2700-rule tool grammar on the first `\-` it meets —
every tool-calling request whose schema set contains such a pattern fails with
"failed to parse grammar". Measured 2026-09-16 against a live Claude Code
session: three rules, one distinct pattern, all requests dead.

The dash needs no escape to BE a dash — this only teaches the parser what the
converter already emits. No grammar that parses today can contain `\-` (it
throws), so nothing that works now can change behavior: the patch accepts a
strict superset, and the superset is exactly one previously-fatal spelling.

